CS 252 Lab 4 – Introduction to Threads
Student: Naman Kansal (kansal2)

FEATURES IMPLEMENTED

Part 1: System Calls
• Implemented a program demonstrating direct system call invocation using syscall()
• Compared output generated using write(), syscall(SYS_write), and printf()
• Used strace to trace and analyze system calls made by the program
• Identified and annotated system calls corresponding to write, syscall, and printf
• Observed differences in system call behavior between library calls and direct syscalls

File: hello-syscall.c
Output analysis: strace.out

---

Part 2: Thread Creation
• Built and executed a multithreaded program using POSIX threads
• Observed interleaved output from multiple threads
• Extended the program to create additional threads printing different characters
• Analyzed thread scheduling behavior and non-deterministic output order
• Examined thread lifecycle and concurrent execution

Files:
• thr1.cc (modified to include additional threads)
• thr2.cc (analyzed behavior and explained output ordering)

---

Part 3: Mutual Exclusion with Mutex Locks
• Executed a shared counter program demonstrating race conditions
• Observed incorrect final counts due to unsynchronized access
• Added pthread mutex locks to protect the critical section
• Ensured correct and consistent final count across multiple runs
• Demonstrated the necessity of mutual exclusion in multithreaded programs

File: count.cc (modified with mutex locks)

---

Part 4: Spin Locks
• Studied a counter program implemented using custom spin locks
• Implemented spin lock and unlock functions using busy waiting
• Used pthread_yield() to improve CPU fairness
• Compared behavior of spin locks with and without yielding
• Measured and analyzed user time, system time, and real time
• Verified correctness of final count after synchronization

File: count_spin.cc

Performance Comparison Table (included in README):
• pthread mutex (count)
• spin lock with pthread_yield
• spin lock without pthread_yield

---

Part 5: Deadlocks
• Executed a program that intentionally caused a deadlock
• Analyzed lock acquisition order leading to deadlock
• Modified the code to eliminate circular wait conditions
• Ensured program terminates correctly without hanging

File: deadlock.cc (fixed version)

---

Part 6: Bounded Buffer (Producer–Consumer)
• Completed a bounded buffer implementation using threads
• Added mutex locks to protect shared buffer access
• Used semaphores to manage buffer capacity and synchronization
• Correctly coordinated producer and consumer threads
• Prevented race conditions and buffer overflow/underflow

File: bounded-buffer.cc

---

Part 7: Buffer Overflow Vulnerability
• Studied a vulnerable C program susceptible to stack buffer overflow
• Exploited the vulnerability by overwriting adjacent memory
• Used little-endian byte ordering to modify a control variable
• Successfully redirected execution to a shell function
• Demonstrated how unsafe string operations lead to security issues
• Explained how the exploit works and why it succeeds

File: buffer.c
Exploit demonstrated via crafted input string

---

CONCEPTS DEMONSTRATED
• System calls and OS interaction
• POSIX thread creation and scheduling
• Race conditions and critical sections
• Mutex locks and spin locks
• Deadlocks and their prevention
• Semaphores and producer–consumer synchronization
• Performance measurement of synchronization primitives
• Buffer overflow vulnerabilities and memory safety
• Importance of secure systems programming

BUILDING AND RUNNING

Compile sources using provided Makefile:
make

Run examples:
./thr1
./count
./count_spin
./deadlock
./bounded-buffer
./buffer <payload>

